{"maxId":101,"entries":{"chapter-101-study-rheumatoid-arthritis-and-other-autoimmune-diseases|Chapter 101 Study: Rheumatoid Arthritis and Other Autoimmune Diseases":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|2010 ACR/EULAR criteria (Table 101-1): joint involvement + serology + acute-phase reactants + duration; score > 6 = definite RA (p1608)Current practice (not the book): the original 2010 ACR/EULAR criteria require a score of ≥6/10 (a score of exactly 6 qualifies) — Aletaha et al., Arthritis Rheum 2010, Table 3.":["u3"],"copy|Criteria cohorts had mean age 46–58 yr — usefulness for diagnosing LORA (late-onset RA, >60 yr) is unvalidated (p1607)":["u4"],"copy|Anti-CCP: 70–78% sensitive, 88–96% specific for RA — more specific than IgM RF, and unlike RF does not rise with normal aging (p1608)":["u5"],"copy|US/Western prevalence ~0.8%; 0.2–0.3% China/Japan/Greece/Africa; up to 7% in some American-Indian/Alaska Native populations (p1608)":["u6"],"copy|Women affected 2–3× more than men overall, but the female predominance narrows in late-onset disease (p1608) — LORA occurs with roughly equal M:F (p1610)":["u7"],"copy|Peak incidence in the 6th–8th decades (p1608); RF prevalence rises with age to ~30% by age 90 (p1610)":["u8"],"copy|Acute onset, oligoarticular (2–6 joints), large/proximal joints, systemic symptoms (weight loss), absent nodules, sicca common, high ESR, often RF-negative":["u9"],"copy|RS3PE syndrome: remitting seronegative symmetric synovitis with pitting edema; older men 2–3:1, negative RF/ANA, responds to low-dose prednisone (p1610–1611)":["u10"],"copy|In one small study, anti-CCP 65% positive in LORA vs 0% in PMR or healthy older adults — may be useful to separate LORA from PMR (p1611)":["u11"],"copy|ACR functional class I–IV (Table 101-3) (p1611)":["u12"],"copy|10 yr RA duration → risk of cervical spine/atlantoaxial instability — assess before intubation (p1612)":["u13"],"copy|Vectra DA: laboratory-developed 12-protein blood test for RA disease activity, complements (does not replace) clinical judgment (p1612; Labcorp Vectra sample report, test 504965, documents its regulatory status)":["u14"],"copy|Rheumatoid nodules in up to 40% of seropositive White patients; pleuritis/pericarditis in 20–30% (usually asymptomatic) (p1613)":["u15"],"copy|Methotrexate monotherapy = first-line for DMARD-naïve moderate-high activity, started without short- or long-term glucocorticoids (2021 ACR guideline) (p1615, p1616)":["u16"],"copy|Hydroxychloroquine dose 5 mg/kg; annual ocular screening, preferably with OCT — avoid in macular degeneration/inoperable dense cataracts (p1614)":["u17"],"copy|Biologic/tsDMARD added if not at target on max-tolerated methotrexate, preferred over triple csDMARD therapy (p1615, p1616)":["u18"],"copy|CONSIDER mnemonic for biologic safety in older adults (Table 101-6): Comorbidities, Opportunistic infections, Novel presentation of side effects, Screening, Immunization, Drug dosing and interactions, Evaluate, Remaining lifespan (p1618)":["u19"],"copy|Glucocorticoid dose-response in Medicare patients aged 75 or older: ≤5 mg, >5–10 mg, >10 mg/day → approximately 20, 32 and 47 hospitalized infections per 100 person-years, respectively, versus 13 per 100 person-years without glucocorticoids (p1618)Current practice (not the book): in the source (Medicare RA patients ≥75 y on stable DMARDs) 12.8/20.0/32.0/46.8 are crude hospitalized-infection rates per 100 person-years, not % increased risk; adjusted HRs vs no glucocorticoid 1.31, 1.74, 2.23 — George et al., Ann Intern Med 2020, Table 3.":["u20"],"copy|TB screening (skin test or IGRA + CXR) required before anticytokine therapy; anti-TNF associated with ~4× malignancy rate in one meta-analysis (p1617)":["u21"],"copy|Late-onset lupus (LOL): onset after age 55, ~10% of all lupus cases (p1619)":["u22"],"copy|UK GPRD: peak incidence in women 50–54 yr, in men 70–74 yr (p1619)":["u23"],"copy|2019 EULAR/ACR criteria (Table 101-7) use ANA ≥1:80 as entry criterion (p1620)":["u24"],"copy|LOL diagnostic delay averages 2–3 years; female:male ratio 4:1 in LOL vs 9:1 in younger-onset lupus (p1621)":["u25"],"copy|LOL symptom frequencies (Table 101-8): arthritis/arthralgia 60%, rash 47%, cytopenias 45%, interstitial pneumonitis 41%, serositis 32%, glomerulonephritis only 24% — milder, less classic disease than YOL (p1621)":["u26"],"copy|Belimumab (anti-BAFF) — first lupus drug in its class to get FDA approval, 2011, indication expanded to adult active lupus nephritis in December 2020; voclosporin (oral calcineurin inhibitor) also approved for active lupus nephritis, role in older patients untested (p1621–1622; FDA supplemental approval letter, December 2020)":["u27"],"copy|Second commonest autoimmune disease after RA; prevalence 0.04–4.8%; peak onset 4th–5th decade, 9:1 F:M (p1622)":["u28"],"copy|Schirmer test positive if <5 mm wetting in 5 min; minor salivary gland biopsy positive if >1 focus (≥50 lymphocytes) per 4 mm²; normal saliva production ≥1.5 mL/15 min (p1622)Current practice (not the book): 2016 ACR/EULAR primary Sjögren criteria use Schirmer ≤5 mm/5 min and focus score ≥1 focus/4 mm² — Shiboski et al., Arthritis Rheumatol 2017.":["u29"],"copy|Extraglandular disease in ~60% (Table 101-9) — nonerosive arthritis/arthralgia is commonest (p1622–1623)":["u30"],"copy|Sjögren patients are 44× more likely to develop lymphoma than matched controls, almost always B-cell, often t(14;18) (p1626)":["u31"],"copy|100 drugs implicated; incidence ~15,000–20,000/yr, 30,000–50,000 affected in the US (p1624–1625)":["u32"],"copy|Procainamide: 1/3 of patients on it >1 yr develop symptoms, almost all ANA+ by 2 yr; hydralazine: <20% develop DIL, risk dose-dependent (>200 mg/day or >100 g cumulative) (p1625)":["u33"],"copy|90% of PIL/HIL patients have antihistone antibodies; IFN-α causes autoimmunity in up to 19% of treated patients (p1625)":["u34"],"copy|Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy = most common malignancy-associated rheumatic syndrome, almost always from metastatic lung cancer (p1625)":["u35"],"copy|Methotrexate-associated lymphoma: ~50% harbor EBV genome, often regresses on drug cessation (p1626)":["u36"],"copy|Azathioprine + cyclosporin A in RA → 2× lymphoma risk; renal transplant on cyclosporin A → 50-fold increased NHL risk (p1626)":["u37"],"copy|Aromatase inhibitors: as many as half of women get subjective joint achiness or pain, rarely frank arthritis (p1626–1627)":["u38"],"copy|Historical vaccine schedule in this edition: mRNA vaccines were preferred to J&J, and an extra dose at least 28 days after the primary series was advised for immunosuppressed patients (p1627–1628)":["u39"],"copy|Rituximab: vaccinate 2–4 weeks before next anticipated dose; most other agents held 1–2 weeks post-vaccination if disease activity allows — but the Task Force reached no consensus on interrupting TNF/IL-6R/IL-1R/IL-17/IL-12,23/IL-23 inhibitors around vaccination (Table 101-11, p1628)":["u40"],"recall|Recall":["u41"],"copy|CHAPTER 101s / HAZZARD":["u42"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 101; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u43"],"copy|RA — definition & classification":["u44"],"copy|RA — epidemiology":["u45"],"copy|LORA clinical pattern (Table 101-2) (p1610)":["u46"],"copy|RA — evaluation":["u47"],"copy|RA — pharmacotherapy (Table 101-4, Table 101-5)":["u48"],"copy|RA — biologics safety & steroids":["u49"],"copy|Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)":["u50"],"copy|Sjögren syndrome":["u51"],"copy|Drug-induced lupus (DIL) (Table 101-10)":["u52"],"copy|Rheumatic syndromes & cancer":["u53"],"copy|COVID-19 and rheumatic disease (Table 101-11)":["u54"],"copy|2.":["u55"],"copy|What are the sensitivity and specificity of anti-CCP antibodies for RA?":["u56"],"copy|70–78% sensitive, 88–96% specific — and unlike RF, anti-CCP incidence does not rise with normal aging (p1608).":["u57"],"copy|3.":["u58"],"copy|How does the male:female ratio of RA differ between typical (younger-onset) RA and LORA?":["u59"],"copy|Typical RA affects women 2–3× more than men, but the female predominance narrows in late-onset disease (p1608) — LORA occurs with roughly equal frequency in men and women (p1610).":["u60"],"copy|4.":["u61"],"copy|What joint pattern and demographic features characterize LORA (Table 101-2)?":["u62"],"copy|Acute-onset oligoarticular disease (2–6 joints), involvement of large/proximal joints, systemic symptoms (e.g., weight loss), absence of rheumatoid nodules, common sicca symptoms, and a roughly 1:1 male:female ratio (p1610).":["u63"],"copy|5.":["u64"],"copy|What is RS3PE syndrome and how does it typically respond to treatment?":["u65"],"copy|Remitting seronegative symmetric synovitis with pitting edema — affects older men (2–3:1), RF/ANA negative, high ESR, and generally responds well to low-dose prednisone (p1610–1611).":["u66"],"copy|8.":["u67"],"copy|At what glucocorticoid doses, and with what relative infection risks, did Medicare beneficiaries over 75 with RA show increased serious-infection rates?":["u68"],"copy|≤5 mg, >5–10 mg, and >10 mg/day of glucocorticoid were associated with approximately 20, 32 and 47 hospitalized infections per 100 person-years, respectively, versus 13 per 100 person-years without glucocorticoids (p1618; George et al., Table 3).":["u69"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): in the source (Medicare RA patients ≥75 y on stable DMARDs) 12.8/20.0/32.0/46.8 are crude hospitalized-infection rates per 100 person-years, not % increased risk; adjusted HRs vs no glucocorticoid 1.31, 1.74, 2.23 — George et al., Ann Intern Med 2020, Table 3.":["u70"],"copy|9.":["u71"],"copy|How is late-onset lupus (LOL) defined, and what fraction of all SLE cases does it represent?":["u72"],"copy|Onset of symptoms after age 55; it represents approximately 10% of all lupus cases (p1619).":["u73"],"copy|10.":["u74"],"copy|How does the female:male ratio of LOL compare with younger-onset lupus?":["u75"],"copy|LOL has a female:male ratio of about 4:1, versus about 9:1 in younger-onset lupus (p1621).":["u76"],"copy|11.":["u77"],"copy|When was belimumab first approved, and when was its indication expanded to lupus nephritis?":["u78"],"copy|FDA-approved in 2011 as the first lupus drug in its class (anti-BAFF); indication expanded to adult active lupus nephritis in December 2020 (p1621–1622; FDA supplemental approval letter, December 2020).":["u79"],"copy|12.":["u80"],"copy|What are the diagnostic thresholds for a positive Schirmer test and a positive minor salivary gland biopsy in Sjögren syndrome?":["u81"],"copy|Schirmer test: less than 5 mm of filter paper wet after 5 minutes.":["u82"],"copy|Minor salivary gland biopsy: more than one focus (an aggregate of ≥50 lymphocytes) per 4 mm² of tissue (p1622).":["u83"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): 2016 ACR/EULAR primary Sjögren criteria use Schirmer ≤5 mm/5 min and focus score ≥1 focus/4 mm² — Shiboski et al., Arthritis Rheumatol 2017.":["u84"],"copy|14.":["u85"],"copy|What dose thresholds define higher risk for hydralazine-induced lupus (HIL)?":["u86"],"copy|Risk is dose-dependent and highest in patients taking more than 200 mg/day or with a cumulative dose over 100 g (p1625).":["u87"],"copy|15.":["u88"],"copy|What is the most common rheumatic syndrome associated with malignancy, and which cancer most often causes it?":["u89"],"copy|Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy — almost always caused by metastatic lung cancer in the Western world (p1625).":["u90"],"copy|Answer":["u91","u92","u93","u94","u95","u96","u97","u98","u99","u100","u101"],"ra-definition-amp-classification|RA — definition & classification":["u44"],"ra-epidemiology|RA — epidemiology":["u45"],"lora-clinical-pattern-table-101-2-p1610|LORA clinical pattern (Table 101-2) (p1610)":["u46"],"ra-evaluation|RA — evaluation":["u47"],"ra-pharmacotherapy-table-101-4-table-101-5|RA — pharmacotherapy (Table 101-4, Table 101-5)":["u48"],"ra-biologics-safety-amp-steroids|RA — biologics safety & steroids":["u49"],"systemic-lupus-erythematosus-sle|Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)":["u50"],"sj-gren-syndrome|Sjögren syndrome":["u51"],"drug-induced-lupus-dil-table-101-10|Drug-induced lupus (DIL) (Table 101-10)":["u52"],"rheumatic-syndromes-amp-cancer|Rheumatic syndromes & cancer":["u53"],"covid-19-and-rheumatic-disease-table-101-11|COVID-19 and rheumatic disease (Table 101-11)":["u54"]}}
